// Luxe Suites curtain wall -- BLACKOUT pane (exterior).
//
// Exists because a3\structures_f_exp\data\glass\glass.rvmat, which build_lux.py
// had been pointing the glass at, IS NOT IN ANY ARMA 3 PBO. Scanned every addon
// in Addons/ and Expansion/Addons/ on 2026-08-08: no such file. With no material
// to load the engine falls back to a default, which ignored the near-black
// diffuse and rendered the tower as glossy grey glass ("the outside windows
// aren't blacked out").
//
// Modelled on the mission's own neon_*.rvmat, the combination already proven to
// ship and render here. diffuse 1,1,1,1 means the assigned texture governs the
// colour -- the pane's tint comes from its procedural
// #(argb,8,8,3)color(0.043,0.067,0.086,0.50,ca), which is the v21 drop's authored
// value (0x0b1116 at alpha 0.50).
//
// WHY THE ALPHA IS NOT THE ONE-WAY DIAL (owner, 2026-08-19: "see through on the
// outside and too dark on the inside"). Alpha is symmetric -- lowering it lets you
// see BOTH ways, raising it blinds BOTH ways. We were on 0.94, which is why the
// interior could not see out; but simply raising it further would black out the
// suites rather than the street.
//
// The exterior read comes from SPECULAR. A raised specular makes the pane throw
// the sky back at a viewer outside, so the glass reflects instead of revealing the
// room, while someone inside -- looking the other way, out of a darker space --
// still sees through. That is how real reflective glazing behaves and it is the
// dial to turn if the balance is still off: raise these three numbers for a more
// mirrored exterior, lower them for more transparency.
//
// forcedDiffuse and emmisive are ZERO: any lift there is what turns a blackout
// pane back into grey glass under a bright sky.
ambient[]={1,1,1,1};
diffuse[]={1,1,1,1};
forcedDiffuse[]={0,0,0,0};
emmisive[]={0,0,0,1};
specular[]={0.30,0.34,0.40,1};
specularPower=140;
PixelShaderID="Normal";
VertexShaderID="Basic";
class StageTI
{
	texture="a3\data_f\default_vehicle_ti_ca.paa";
};
